# Prime review — return-review packet assembler 05.01

Date: 2026-10-29. Reviewer: Codex Prime.
Disposition: accepted as a bounded mechanical lab helper, not seated app runtime.

Prime independently ran ten tests: nine executed, one file-symlink privilege skip,
no failures. All 19 artifact-manifest rows match current bytes. Capture 0167 freshly
verified complete. Prime then actually used the helper to gather packets for 0166
and 0167 into a separate temporary output directory. Both assemblies completed and
preserved exact returns and custody facts without accepting or dispatching work.
This is actual Prime tool use, not a token-saving or speed claim.

Exit 0 means assembly complete, not return complete or accepted. Packet readers
must inspect the custody, source and artifact statuses. The helper supports only
the explicit artifact-manifest/1 contract for artifact rows. It correctly leaves
0164's prime-entry-pin-artifact-manifest/1 unsupported. Its historical 0163 example
separates committed artifacts from a changed working copy; a later authorized edit
is not corruption of the historical return. Unknown schemas remain unverified.
The readable packet points to exact source snapshots rather than replacing them
with a generated semantic summary. Prime still supplies sources and judgment.

The assignment said both not to open Claude's unfinished work and to compare
changed live artifacts. Those instructions were in tension for the 0163 example.
The Builder retained live digests rather than copied live code; do not describe
that as proving no live observation occurred. Future actual review packets should
use committed or captured artifacts by default and require an explicit reason for
live comparisons. No source payload or recorded command is executed by the helper.

No extension or assistant API is required for this slice's finish. Prime can use
the accepted helper with explicit requests; wider app integration is future work.
No OS sandbox, race safety, automatic selection or semantic acceptance is claimed.
